\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Two failure topologies. In (a), the observation provides no discrimination, so the confusability graph is a clique: every state is confusable with every other, and exact recovery requires a tag for each state. In (b), partial observations provide some discrimination: opposite corners are distinguishable even though adjacent states are not. The 4-cycle is 2-colorable, so exact recovery requires only 2 tags. This structural difference is invisible to the unit-rate threshold alone.}}{7}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:failure-topology}{{1}{7}{Two failure topologies. In (a), the observation provides no discrimination, so the confusability graph is a clique: every state is confusable with every other, and exact recovery requires a tag for each state. In (b), partial observations provide some discrimination: opposite corners are distinguishable even though adjacent states are not. The 4-cycle is 2-colorable, so exact recovery requires only 2 tags. This structural difference is invisible to the unit-rate threshold alone}{figure.1}{}}
